#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233755 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0bf78498-fa41-5c5b-8e73-bc357aed8c2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-29825" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{-2}\\right)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{x}^{-2}+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d88db36a-1743-57b4-81de-ba3e94f146e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d88db36a-1743-57b4-81de-ba3e94f146e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232540} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf0faa2-183e-595b-9158-aab3d3891b84" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a902a53e-f058-534a-bee4-004868eb968b" :question/math-academy-id "q-310188" :question/problem "Differentiate ${3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x).$\n\n$({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({3}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3))(\\operatorname{sin}(x))+({3}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233776 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d79a7bd8-521e-5538-bc94-8c3a64e6621d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31226" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tan}x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})({sec}^{2}x)+(\\operatorname{tan}x)(6x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\pi } & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x{|}_{x=\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi )^{2}{sec}^{2}(\\pi )+6(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 1+6\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3{\\pi }^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131acbe9-558d-5eca-b043-6011320bbf1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "131acbe9-558d-5eca-b043-6011320bbf1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232577} :answer-field/id #uuid "db20f675-f846-587b-8b98-472e5b4e4ce4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04a32680-52e9-52f1-b831-14d1889bed06" :question/math-academy-id "q-319402" :question/problem "Differentiate $(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x)$\n\n$((2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'}=-{sec}^{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2-\\operatorname{tan}(x)$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(x)+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(-{sec}^{2}(x))(\\operatorname{cos}(x))+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))(-\\operatorname{sin}(x)) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)+(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e6108cd-c0ec-571c-ad0a-13dc2e7b511f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232579} {:answer/id #uuid "dec63f26-4bc5-4a1b-82e6-38b3bd6341a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670944} {:answer/id #uuid "040e6cf7-0789-41fd-a4fe-d511c15e7a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670945} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9a1076-00fc-4cab-9ade-a3329f1c19bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x\\ln" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670946} {:answer/id #uuid "32150198-ed6d-42a1-bb94-405669a0f294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x\\ln5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670947} {:answer/id #uuid "18b41c1b-22a4-48fe-a0ca-2f2261787e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x\\ln5\\sin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670948} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce6a3cf-71ad-414a-988a-bed5e2a378f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x\\ln5\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e6108cd-c0ec-571c-ad0a-13dc2e7b511f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232579} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d91600c-0002-59f3-81a1-16e9bcfd0e5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11843f86-fdb6-56e0-8408-0d8387a30533" :question/math-academy-id "q-319421" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={5}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={({5}^{x})}^{'}\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}{(\\operatorname{sin}(x))}^{'} \\\\  & =({5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5))\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9bc784-4648-528a-ac26-6d3516a71384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b9bc784-4648-528a-ac26-6d3516a71384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232589} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a2fdd90-ea5c-5dc2-bea7-c9bda09ae803" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c831400-ba54-50fb-91a6-b9b14e55efca" :question/math-academy-id "q-319825" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-1)}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{2(0-1)}{2(0)+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(-2)+\\operatorname{ln}(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb3cf46d-4dfa-538f-ba3e-cfde7c2bd873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb3cf46d-4dfa-538f-ba3e-cfde7c2bd873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232591} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac9d63dd-c99e-5f08-8d1a-09b6cb6e69fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab210aae-e5d2-52de-b35d-cb8603b929bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-319909" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x},$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=2x{e}^{2x}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}{e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}({e}^{2x})^{'} \\\\  & =(2x)({e}^{2x})+({x}^{2})(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234106 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6c1d13e-58bd-57d8-918b-0a1bebf09fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49173" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {(1+5{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+5{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+10{x}^{2}+25{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+10\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)+10\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+(x)+C \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}+\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{3}+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234186 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48cc16d0-0280-564d-bcf3-e481ad8d20ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-66126" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 1\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we write the integrand as a power of $x$, we have ${x}^{0}=1.$ Therefore, we can use the power rule with $n=0$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= 1+0 \\\\ &= 1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234296 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b5dea81-9340-51d4-993f-9f9ce501ae79" :question/math-academy-id "q-71004" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{sin}x)+({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234298 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c9da781-1702-5ac7-9948-600ab4559823" :question/math-academy-id "q-71096" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{5/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21fbeb51-6dc3-543d-a41d-b167ecce3c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{-6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513623} {:answer/id #uuid "3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513684} {:answer/id #uuid "47d643d4-e671-5e72-9cf0-0a89cbdb8d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{1/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513726} {:answer/id #uuid "979719be-a5ae-506b-afac-4bb48471132a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{5/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513925} {:answer/id #uuid "b3abc2ef-10c8-500f-8329-7a3eb25bffce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513684} :answer-field/id #uuid "f072598f-e1c5-599b-affc-4413eaf94190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "44529618-fcf7-5581-8bd3-a0df68de242f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71100" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234300 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eb47b292-deba-58b8-a79e-0386e8ea55e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71121" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=10$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{10+1}}{10+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{11}\\cdot 11{x}^{10} \\\\ &= {x}^{10}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80a672a5-9018-5a78-9672-cf47be4d95f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}+3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566608} {:answer/id #uuid "98e55f7b-e970-5ec1-8f87-371c4a6f55f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}-4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566663} {:answer/id #uuid "d87f3a3b-f1ff-5e22-81b2-679daf615e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566796} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4e8d2e-8e7f-55f8-887c-4c3c6d134e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{x}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566840} {:answer/id #uuid "f5261de7-027d-545e-bff9-4ea8c302891e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5261de7-027d-545e-bff9-4ea8c302891e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566855} :answer-field/id #uuid "f689fbb7-4820-549d-86af-2dc10f9a0677" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79aa6724-ce36-5977-907b-506fab54c4d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71169" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\text{d}x+4\\int {x}^{0}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1/2}+4x+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234303 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09d58888-0116-53f3-b47a-9d0d7153229f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71173" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2+\\frac{1}{{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2+\\frac{1}{{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y+\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y+\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}\\right)+\\frac{{y}^{-5+1}}{-5+1}+C \\\\  & =2y+\\frac{{y}^{-4}}{-4}+C \\\\  & =2y-\\frac{1}{4{y}^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234309 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f9e9368-db2a-5195-a33c-14fd53ecbddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-71653" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\text{d}y=\\operatorname{sin}y+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 4\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\text{d}y & =4\\int \\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}y)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}y+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234310 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea9f76a8-e76e-5423-a193-fa42c402c584" :question/math-academy-id "q-71654" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\operatorname{sin}x+5\\operatorname{cos}x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C, \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{sin}x+5\\operatorname{cos}x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\,\\text{d}x+5\\int \\operatorname{cos}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+5\\operatorname{sin}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234311 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27d3e02d-3099-56f8-b860-c654c168397f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71657" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-8\\operatorname{csc}y\\,\\operatorname{cot}y)\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{csc}y\\operatorname{cot}y\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\operatorname{csc}y+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-8\\operatorname{csc}y\\,\\operatorname{cot}y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-8\\int \\operatorname{csc}y\\operatorname{cot}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-8(-\\operatorname{csc}y)+C \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{csc}y+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f0c4bf-c884-5ffe-9bbb-fd66e54891c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f0c4bf-c884-5ffe-9bbb-fd66e54891c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19367e3-97ce-5783-bf46-3e1a0dadc9a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c85a07f-1862-53e2-9986-87c4ee966897" :question/math-academy-id "q-132983" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{t}^{2}+3)^{4})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}\\right)+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead734d2-e970-5e92-9ac3-1260bbfab982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead734d2-e970-5e92-9ac3-1260bbfab982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234918} :answer-field/id #uuid "3429cda5-daf9-53dc-9c18-72e4afbade0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dabe687a-249f-595c-be70-5214c92992e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167326" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f74b2bd2-a95c-5b62-907c-ae6cda7b7089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f74b2bd2-a95c-5b62-907c-ae6cda7b7089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234936} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e8b47e0-d44a-50a7-b006-cdcb0702528f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19dc50e5-0f97-5ac3-ba2b-ebf4a9bbf79f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167331" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\n$${{field-1}}$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234897} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa3c58-8fd9-5d31-988a-048384e9e06f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cee13c2e-6226-5f1c-9ea2-cd4611314d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-167564" :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d971a67d-9a39-5728-97d4-88d7a1a29d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cot}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d971a67d-9a39-5728-97d4-88d7a1a29d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cot}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234886} :answer-field/id #uuid "bed829ad-3fbc-5ade-9d74-02d9be633d41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6851a62-df7c-598a-8ba2-7eecd95e5c54" :question/math-academy-id "q-167569" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$+\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd74cf7-7832-5d4d-9bdb-4352f71d9f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbd74cf7-7832-5d4d-9bdb-4352f71d9f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234944} :answer-field/id #uuid "6614a1f6-a87a-5480-bfb4-b31cd6dddad3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c736f2-25bd-5d43-acf7-389cd352b94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44c736f2-25bd-5d43-acf7-389cd352b94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234650} :answer-field/id #uuid "86dd5a39-356d-58f6-a814-f6ba7ef31193" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235139} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd151ce5-c877-5cb0-954c-57ccd397f03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd151ce5-c877-5cb0-954c-57ccd397f03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234837} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fb953ef-938a-5b5b-99c5-5fa2a0878578" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235145} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80153a7b-80ae-54f5-b4ee-35a981cc0cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80153a7b-80ae-54f5-b4ee-35a981cc0cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234740} :answer-field/id #uuid "914f87c0-b0d8-5ddd-be80-6c4d6303ec75" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb796ec5-fb4b-5f50-95bf-f4781e9dc4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb796ec5-fb4b-5f50-95bf-f4781e9dc4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234943} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed723404-e7d9-5542-afad-7590ee940dcb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed1a25d3-8778-5a6a-8c12-425fa1c7957d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167631" :question/problem "Complete the following facts about the function $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe domain of the function is $x\\in ($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$).$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe range of the function is $[$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-4}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$].$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The period of the function is\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-5}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n![](images/96/96bd60d23237f836b77a26c5c0461a5c2a474554f0a14266b53fc5e7d999f1b7.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n* The domain of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n* The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$ Therefore, the range is $[-1,1].$\n\n* The period of $sin⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e6b660-e7fa-5392-b0f6-a50bdfd47004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2/x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49e6b660-e7fa-5392-b0f6-a50bdfd47004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2/x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234659} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ba49d8-2db6-5100-8d54-7ea280ac61ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "221f7f46-e501-5c4b-9474-541896c7450d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167635" :question/problem "Given that $A=2$ and $B=3,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C.$" :question/worked-solution "We found that $A=2$ and $B=3$ in steps 2 and 3, respectively. Hence, $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x.$ \n\nUsing the sum and constant factor rules and our result from step 4, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbf988f2-8940-5547-8a0c-461e7fba4865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbf988f2-8940-5547-8a0c-461e7fba4865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234891} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6fb57fa-80a8-5893-88ed-795c73c49674" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8af2c303-b3aa-5790-885e-7a978a609bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167780" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e925cb33-a34c-536e-9ec6-fc4ac693e32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e925cb33-a34c-536e-9ec6-fc4ac693e32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234915} :answer-field/id #uuid "747ebec0-6972-5890-bd56-72dc24b81b4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6da96d34-46d1-5bca-95e5-7bacca66a935" :question/math-academy-id "q-167785" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\n$${{field-1}}$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090fc51b-4d12-5f60-88f8-ac5a4b13097b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461255} {:answer/id #uuid "4a490cbe-a1fc-5779-866a-04511c2d89df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a490cbe-a1fc-5779-866a-04511c2d89df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461256} :answer-field/id #uuid "4660fa26-6eb4-552c-b659-a5bb3fdb38e2" :answer-field/key "$3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203315ab-b883-5cf3-9e0f-dc0d51fd720c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461257} {:answer/id #uuid "2140d6dd-0dab-5e0c-9b15-f877af212e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2140d6dd-0dab-5e0c-9b15-f877af212e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461258} :answer-field/id #uuid "e00a579e-e820-57de-ab22-60410abdcf00" :answer-field/key "$3{y}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462382} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba813b06-d5fe-5f56-8fdc-f1ae37721250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461259} {:answer/id #uuid "19534c2b-71f9-5560-90e2-02c8041640df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19534c2b-71f9-5560-90e2-02c8041640df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461260} :answer-field/id #uuid "19628958-a21d-5f53-ae89-d685f932baef" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59bba89f-5fae-5f25-a8be-5a25060edfd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103172" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2})=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$ gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$; $3{y}^{2}$; $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6f503f6-69a7-5d68-a11b-13f00bdd16f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461261} {:answer/id #uuid "848dd2d6-6e77-557c-b98a-f43e6b7db9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6f503f6-69a7-5d68-a11b-13f00bdd16f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461261} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc41026f-42e8-5b54-9417-9d6b477e89f8" :answer-field/key "$6x{y}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462384} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f1fd05-76fc-5b99-a0c2-a90083805755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461263} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1f0dd1-4635-59a8-8716-ef1056569f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f1f0dd1-4635-59a8-8716-ef1056569f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461264} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b91d6b1-05d5-5e41-9dba-2acad420c227" :answer-field/key "$3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462385} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106f08f9-0740-5a25-acd7-bad0545e6965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461265} {:answer/id #uuid "427fad5f-6fec-57c1-9ac0-73467afa5d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "106f08f9-0740-5a25-acd7-bad0545e6965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461265} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1e2b2a-3495-573d-8c25-cae8e1c5c279" :answer-field/key "$2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d411ceca-268e-5683-adfb-764387aebbdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103174" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{2})=6{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''$3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ only''."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6cba8d6-c415-53ab-badb-6bf8dbbbd0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461267} {:answer/id #uuid "319d8d6d-e36c-5d92-9294-5c3e0ebc3a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "319d8d6d-e36c-5d92-9294-5c3e0ebc3a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461268} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d85e36-2f10-523d-8a99-52889142a7c1" :answer-field/key "${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7b07408-8bfc-50ff-a374-c9d5ffa3c8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461269} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7fd223-bf94-5026-8a1f-ea7d0c2bf194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7b07408-8bfc-50ff-a374-c9d5ffa3c8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461269} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5efdf9e-1d50-589f-8cc5-55517cb057ba" :answer-field/key "$-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40734f81-131a-5471-b8b3-e03ea290a58a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461271} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6b4205-de62-57e6-aad8-a56544ae9904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40734f81-131a-5471-b8b3-e03ea290a58a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461271} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b089f27-1db2-55de-a28d-d2d13e8068fe" :answer-field/key "${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ffb478e-6d0e-5395-bbb6-0e1bbace63ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-103175" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ only''."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdd8d5ad-073e-55ee-92c1-3d941cfbf8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437300} {:answer/id #uuid "62442bed-3629-535b-844a-f1ed8cc3a547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62442bed-3629-535b-844a-f1ed8cc3a547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437301} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b115472-4d06-5a5e-9cd3-15210c6010e5" :answer-field/key "The function is zero at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7edfcf80-e21f-5e22-8902-7e7b1332485b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437302} {:answer/id #uuid "9c74c49e-6c9c-5bab-9fb2-8efb50f61131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7edfcf80-e21f-5e22-8902-7e7b1332485b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437302} :answer-field/id #uuid "9da1c4d9-8e1c-5e3d-8925-a2ad4ef01523" :answer-field/key "Its range is $y\\in [-2,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57a3617f-5ddc-5f1e-9587-578cb2e5daae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437304} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3927d0-6855-5216-a9b2-c6bb7f951391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57a3617f-5ddc-5f1e-9587-578cb2e5daae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437304} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42ef86b-359c-5579-8280-6358310897d5" :answer-field/key "$y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is an output of the function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4817b633-1cdd-52d9-b9a7-098236a7867f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103230" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is zero at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$” is true. At $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, the function $y=cos⁡x$ takes the value zero.\n\n- Statements “Its range is $y\\in [-2,2]$” and “$y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is an output of the function” are false. The range of the function $y=cos⁡x$ is $y\\in [-1,1].$ Thus, $y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is not an output of this function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54f7039a-2f3c-5e11-b655-d32aed6af515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437306} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e0e83d-6b7c-5018-bd20-49f606eedb04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54f7039a-2f3c-5e11-b655-d32aed6af515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437306} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78118cd-b206-5d3b-aac4-77a392c6c22c" :answer-field/key "The zeros of the function are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fbfbd2d-9a45-5a4b-b854-d3e63ee7830d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437308} {:answer/id #uuid "0c07a39f-6574-5ab6-9301-59c89b690d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c07a39f-6574-5ab6-9301-59c89b690d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437309} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b3d79d-1762-5848-9642-6db59cbc38e2" :answer-field/key "Its graph passes through the point $(0,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efed1b10-8121-5609-a029-df7005434c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437310} {:answer/id #uuid "82200c6e-534d-5c90-9855-36c2db1fd8ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efed1b10-8121-5609-a029-df7005434c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437310} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c76314-0f5c-552b-9634-7a579528542d" :answer-field/key "The function has maximum values at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4afb86c8-970f-594e-bb88-36f6e2c3e725" :question/math-academy-id "q-103233" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The zeros of the function are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi $” is false. The zeros occur at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…,\n$$ which can be written as $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “Its graph passes through the point $(0,1)$” is true. At $x=0,$ the function has the value $y=cos⁡0=1.$ Therefore, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Statement “The function has maximum values at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$” is false. The function has a maximum at $x=0,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=2n\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe07cef-135a-5aee-b857-d8421aa48542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235900} {:answer/id #uuid "13614331-5578-515d-8943-8af84bd83334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235929} {:answer/id #uuid "89e2a1f7-298a-5958-86d7-db27000a04bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236680} {:answer/id #uuid "bb07dd52-f518-54f6-8f86-fe701c1ada44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237014} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f1e392-4f36-5478-9ad8-f088d40891d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2f1e392-4f36-5478-9ad8-f088d40891d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237242} :answer-field/id #uuid "9baaf465-f7d4-5147-8968-33136247a92f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4d276077-f8d6-5c9d-ba5b-27234154bb37" :question/math-academy-id "q-103242" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-\\frac{5}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x+12\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=6$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot 6\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x+12\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=-\\frac{5}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x+12\\pi )=-\\frac{5}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215fe18b-9f23-5c09-bf88-127529513e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236030} {:answer/id #uuid "706dc48b-2178-5af0-963c-8392b5818616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236528} {:answer/id #uuid "852258c8-e623-5916-a3ff-e2ea52c2dac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236648} {:answer/id #uuid "dab49c4d-12af-58e7-98db-dab868211f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237203} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d0f57a-a36f-5ba2-9697-d3011f1d0734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "706dc48b-2178-5af0-963c-8392b5818616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236528} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c72a16-c41c-5f7d-a59f-cf1e101272bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c027c724-5ec4-54a9-b208-10f063932ce3" :question/math-academy-id "q-103243" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x-14\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot (-7)\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x-14\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14927c29-0886-540d-95a2-4473de589ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235940} {:answer/id #uuid "42b67f48-9900-5969-bb84-e376aa0e142a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236244} {:answer/id #uuid "b71cb3ce-28e0-585f-9894-ebfd9f88d9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236978} {:answer/id #uuid "bae2f1a4-b12f-55b2-94e9-674f5ea009e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237013} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3be85b-3abb-5d86-a3c0-ca3e422113ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14927c29-0886-540d-95a2-4473de589ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235940} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f2a3ce-c6d5-5bd3-a262-29e4efe476df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4c5a6195-b8ed-52d4-86b4-4695109267db" :question/math-academy-id "q-103279" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{3}{8},$ then $4cos⁡(x-10\\pi )+5=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x-10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{3}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x-10\\pi )=\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4cos⁡(x-10\\pi )+5 & =4\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
